  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1028 AM MDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nThe drying trend continues today as the upper high settles to the \neast over the Southern Plains. However, PWs remain above normal, \nespecially out west with values of 1.3-1.5\" modeled for this \nafternoon. The inverted trough that gave us scattered to widespread \nconvection and isolated flash flooding earlier this week will push \nnorthward through the Four Corners today. In its wake will be \nisolated showers and storms, focusing over area mtns with slow storm \nmotion to the north. Recent heavy rainfall in the Sacs has saturated \nthe soils, heightening the flood risk today. Rainfall up to 1.5\" is \npossible according to the HREF/REFS, favoring the area around \nMescalero in the early afternoon, where impacts have been most \nsevere this week unfortunately. Most areas should be dry today with \nspotty lowland showers and light southerly winds.\n\nPWs fall to about 1\" tomorrow as high pressure aloft drifts \nwestward, keeping most of us dry with the exception of isolated \nshowers/storms in area mtns and out west. Rainfall rates should \ndecrease for Thu. Temps will continue to climb with lowland highs of \n95-100F. \n\nThe upper high holds steady over the Four Corners/Southern Rockies \ninto next week, keeping temps above normal. Lowland highs of 100-\n105F are forecast from Fri onward with potential Heat Advisories for \nthe lower RGV, including El Paso. Global ensembles are signaling an \nincrease in moisture/inverted trough to reach our area for the \nweekend with PWs around 1.2-1.3\", maybe lingering into the middle \nof next week. Area mtns and western areas will be favored for \nstorms over the weekend with isolated lowland activity in the \nevening hours. Storm chances look to diminish late in the period.",
